WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.847
Part one defined divides.

2
00:00:05.847 --> 00:00:09.266
Now three words built on it.

3
00:00:09.266 --> 00:00:12.567
Even: a equals two times c

4
00:00:12.567 --> 00:00:14.925
for some integer c.

5
00:00:14.925 --> 00:00:18.462
Odd: a equals two k plus one

6
00:00:18.462 --> 00:00:20.820
for some integer k.

7
00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:25.132
And prime, which we come to later.

8
00:00:25.132 --> 00:00:26.763
First a test.

9
00:00:26.763 --> 00:00:28.977
Is minus five odd?

10
00:00:28.977 --> 00:00:32.356
Which integer k do you need?

11
00:00:32.356 --> 00:00:34.570
Pause and find it.

12
00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:41.178
Minus five is two times minus three

13
00:00:41.178 --> 00:00:42.019
plus one.

14
00:00:42.019 --> 00:00:45.300
Not minus two: that gives minus three.

15
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.151
So minus five is odd

16
00:00:47.151 --> 00:00:49.590
with k equal to minus three.

17
00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:53.023
Zero is even: zero is two times zero.

18
00:00:53.023 --> 00:00:55.023
Minus fourteen is even

19
00:00:55.023 --> 00:00:56.940
two times minus seven.

20
00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:58.607
On the number line

21
00:00:58.607 --> 00:01:02.357
the even numbers are steps of two from zero

22
00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:04.357
and the odd numbers are

23
00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:07.940
steps of two from one, in both directions.

24
00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:09.245
With dots

25
00:01:09.245 --> 00:01:13.543
an even number of dots makes two equal rows.

26
00:01:13.543 --> 00:01:16.599
Thirteen makes two rows of six

27
00:01:16.599 --> 00:01:18.796
and one dot left over.

28
00:01:18.796 --> 00:01:22.330
Thirteen is two times six, plus one.

29
00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:24.194
Now a proof.

30
00:01:24.194 --> 00:01:28.793
If a and b are odd, then a times b is odd.

31
00:01:28.793 --> 00:01:31.895
Unwrap: a is two x plus one

32
00:01:31.895 --> 00:01:34.569
and b is two y plus one

33
00:01:34.569 --> 00:01:37.350
with new letters x and y.

34
00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:40.937
The goal is an integer k with

35
00:01:41.048 --> 00:01:44.173
a b equal to two k plus one.

36
00:01:44.173 --> 00:01:48.191
Multiply out: four x y, plus two x

37
00:01:48.191 --> 00:01:50.646
plus two y, plus one.

38
00:01:50.646 --> 00:01:55.334
That is two times, two x y plus x plus y

39
00:01:55.334 --> 00:01:56.450
plus one.

40
00:01:56.450 --> 00:02:01.026
And two x y plus x plus y is an integer.

41
00:02:01.026 --> 00:02:02.700
So a b is odd.

42
00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:04.012
For example

43
00:02:04.012 --> 00:02:06.659
five times seven is thirty-five.

44
00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:09.065
In a five by seven rectangle

45
00:02:09.065 --> 00:02:11.391
the first four rows pair up.

46
00:02:11.391 --> 00:02:13.796
The last row has three pairs

47
00:02:13.796 --> 00:02:15.641
and one dot left over

48
00:02:15.641 --> 00:02:18.528
thirty-five is two times seventeen

49
00:02:18.528 --> 00:02:19.330
plus one.

50
00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:23.873
Can a number be both even and odd?

51
00:02:23.873 --> 00:02:29.118
Suppose a is two c, and also two k plus one.

52
00:02:29.118 --> 00:02:33.430
Then two times c minus k equals one.

53
00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:35.745
Call c minus k, j.

54
00:02:35.745 --> 00:02:40.113
It is an integer, and two j equals one.

55
00:02:40.113 --> 00:02:42.951
But if j is zero or less

56
00:02:42.951 --> 00:02:45.463
two j is zero or less.

57
00:02:45.463 --> 00:02:48.192
And if j is one or more

58
00:02:48.192 --> 00:02:50.703
two j is at least two.

59
00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:52.996
One sits in the gap.

60
00:02:52.996 --> 00:02:55.180
No integer j works.

61
00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:57.375
So no integer is both.

62
00:02:57.459 --> 00:02:59.639
That every integer is even

63
00:02:59.723 --> 00:03:03.412
or odd is also true, but its proof waits for

64
00:03:03.496 --> 00:03:06.850
the division theorem, in lecture eleven.

65
00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:08.519
Now primes.

66
00:03:08.519 --> 00:03:11.588
A positive integer p is prime

67
00:03:11.588 --> 00:03:14.756
provided p is greater than one

68
00:03:14.855 --> 00:03:17.825
and its only positive divisors

69
00:03:17.924 --> 00:03:19.310
are one and p.

70
00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:23.018
In dots, a divisor is a number of rows

71
00:03:23.107 --> 00:03:25.493
that the dots fill exactly.

72
00:03:25.493 --> 00:03:28.762
Twelve dots make three rows of four

73
00:03:28.762 --> 00:03:30.530
or two rows of six.

74
00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:32.474
Three divides twelve

75
00:03:32.562 --> 00:03:35.125
and three is strictly between

76
00:03:35.214 --> 00:03:36.539
one and twelve.

77
00:03:36.539 --> 00:03:38.660
So twelve is composite.

78
00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:41.022
Thirteen dots make only

79
00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:44.259
one kind of rectangle: a single row.

80
00:03:44.259 --> 00:03:48.108
Its positive divisors are one and thirteen.

81
00:03:48.108 --> 00:03:49.770
Thirteen is prime.

82
00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:52.493
What about ninety-one?

83
00:03:52.493 --> 00:03:56.584
It is odd, it is not a multiple of three

84
00:03:56.584 --> 00:03:59.312
and not a multiple of five.

85
00:03:59.312 --> 00:04:00.870
It looks prime.

86
00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:03.629
But ninety-one dots make

87
00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:06.038
seven rows of thirteen.

88
00:04:06.038 --> 00:04:09.652
Ninety-one is seven times thirteen

89
00:04:09.652 --> 00:04:11.660
so it is composite.

90
00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:14.571
Looking prime is not a test.

91
00:04:14.571 --> 00:04:16.980
Only the definition is.

92
00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:18.271
And one?

93
00:04:18.271 --> 00:04:19.871
One is not prime

94
00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:22.360
because the definition asks

95
00:04:22.449 --> 00:04:24.493
for p greater than one.

96
00:04:24.493 --> 00:04:26.982
It is not composite either

97
00:04:27.071 --> 00:04:29.738
there is no integer b with one

98
00:04:29.827 --> 00:04:32.138
less than b less than one.

99
00:04:32.138 --> 00:04:33.560
One is neither.

100
00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:37.958
Two is prime, and it is the only even prime.

101
00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:40.810
Any larger even number has two

102
00:04:40.902 --> 00:04:43.110
as a divisor in between.

103
00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:45.780
One more definition.

104
00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:49.437
A positive integer a is composite

105
00:04:49.437 --> 00:04:52.258
provided some b divides a

106
00:04:52.258 --> 00:04:55.810
with one less than b less than a.

107
00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:59.375
Theorem: for every a at least two

108
00:04:59.469 --> 00:05:01.927
a is composite if and only

109
00:05:02.022 --> 00:05:03.724
if a is not prime.

110
00:05:03.724 --> 00:05:07.789
So every such a is exactly one of the two.

111
00:05:07.789 --> 00:05:11.855
Here are two to seven, sorted: two, three

112
00:05:11.855 --> 00:05:14.313
five and seven are prime

113
00:05:14.313 --> 00:05:16.960
four and six are composite.

114
00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:21.375
The key step: if a positive b divides a

115
00:05:21.375 --> 00:05:24.118
with a equal to b times c

116
00:05:24.118 --> 00:05:28.488
then c is at least one, so b is at most a.

117
00:05:28.488 --> 00:05:31.638
That fact is in no definition.

118
00:05:31.638 --> 00:05:33.670
We had to prove it.

119
00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:35.249
To sum up.

120
00:05:35.249 --> 00:05:39.444
Even is two c, and odd is two k plus one

121
00:05:39.444 --> 00:05:42.240
for zero and negatives too.

122
00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:46.135
The product of two odd numbers is odd.

123
00:05:46.135 --> 00:05:50.130
And one is neither prime nor composite.

124
00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:52.091
Try it yourself

125
00:05:52.091 --> 00:05:56.419
show that n squared plus n is always even.

126
00:05:56.419 --> 00:05:59.339
Next lecture: what if, then

127
00:05:59.339 --> 00:06:02.560
and if and only if really mean.
